ay J2, Play B7, Play F1, Play T2, Play B7, Play T3, Peng W2, Play F2, Play J1, Play T6, Peng W4, Play W5, Play T4, Play T5, Play W6, Play T1, BuGang W2, Play W7, Play J3.\nThe followings are your valid actions:[Play T6; Play B5; Play T3; Play W7; Play B6; Play T7; Play F2; Play T8]\u3002", "reasoning": "", "output": "Evaluation: \nLet me analyze my current hand and potential winning patterns.\n\n**Current Hand:** W7, T3, T3, T6, T7, T7, T8, B5, B5, B6, F2\n**Existing Meld:** [T5, T6, T7]\n\nKey observations:\n1. I have potential for **Half Flush** (6 points) - I have many Bamboo tiles: T3(2), T6, T7(2), T8, plus the meld [T5,T6,T7]. If I can complete this pattern with honor tiles, it would be valuable.\n\n2. **Current tile groupings:**\n   - T3, T3 (pair)\n   - T6, T7, T7, T8 (can form T6-T7-T8 chow, leaving T7)\n   - B5, B5, B6 (B5 pair + B6)\n   - W7, F2 (isolated tiles)\n\n3. **Waiting analysis:**\n   - If I discard W7 and F2 (non-Bamboo tiles), I move closer to Half Flush\n   - Looking at unshown tiles: T4(3), T5(2), T7(2), T8(2) are still available\n   - B4(4), B5(3), B6(2), B7(1) for Dot suit\n\n4. **Best discards to pursue Half Flush:**\n   - **W7** - Character suit, doesn't fit Half Flush pattern\n   - **F2** - Honor tile, but only 1 copy, doesn't contribute to multiple patterns\n   - **B5 or B6** - These break potential Dot suit sequences\n\n5. **Tile efficiency:**\n   - Discarding W7 keeps maximum flexibility for Bamboo-based patterns\n   - W7 has only 1 unshown copy remaining, making it hard to use\n   - F2 has 2 unshown but doesn't fit well with current hand structure\n\n**Most promising path:** Pursue Half Flush by eliminating Character and keeping flexible Bamboo combinations. T3(2), T6, T7(2), T8 can reorganize into various chows.\n\nReason: \nW7 is an isolated Character tile that doesn't contribute to any promising pattern. With only 1 copy unshown, it's unlikely to form a useful combination. Discarding it maintains my path toward Half Flush (6 point